WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.764
A café sells tea, coffee and juice.

2
00:00:05.764 --> 00:00:08.465
It keeps its sales in a table X

3
00:00:08.465 --> 00:00:11.492
one row per day, Monday to Thursday

4
00:00:11.492 --> 00:00:13.620
and one column per drink.

5
00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:16.371
Draw the table as a block

6
00:00:16.371 --> 00:00:19.972
three columns wide and four rows tall.

7
00:00:19.972 --> 00:00:23.295
The top row is Monday: twelve teas

8
00:00:23.295 --> 00:00:26.250
thirty coffees and five juices.

9
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:28.718
One row per sample and one

10
00:00:28.799 --> 00:00:30.347
column per feature.

11
00:00:30.428 --> 00:00:33.116
That is the shape of nearly every

12
00:00:33.198 --> 00:00:35.560
data set in machine learning.

13
00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:39.682
Turning the table on its side is a mirror

14
00:00:39.769 --> 00:00:42.188
image in this diagonal line.

15
00:00:42.188 --> 00:00:44.953
Keep a faint copy of the block

16
00:00:44.953 --> 00:00:47.372
and reflect it in the line.

17
00:00:47.458 --> 00:00:49.791
Now it is four columns wide

18
00:00:49.878 --> 00:00:53.420
and three rows tall: that is X transpose.

19
00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:57.378
Monday's row has become the first column.

20
00:00:57.378 --> 00:01:00.898
Every other entry crosses the line too

21
00:01:00.898 --> 00:01:03.802
thirty five coffees on Thursday

22
00:01:03.802 --> 00:01:07.586
in row four, column two, lands in row two

23
00:01:07.586 --> 00:01:08.730
column four.

24
00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:11.278
In symbols, entry i

25
00:01:11.278 --> 00:01:14.686
j of the transpose is entry j

26
00:01:14.686 --> 00:01:16.774
i of the original.

27
00:01:16.774 --> 00:01:21.720
A four by three table becomes three by four.

28
00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:26.262
Now a small matrix that acts on the plane.

29
00:01:26.262 --> 00:01:29.657
F has rows three, one and two, one.

30
00:01:29.657 --> 00:01:33.902
It sends the two unit arrows to its columns

31
00:01:33.902 --> 00:01:36.260
three, two and one, one.

32
00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:38.561
Go back to the start

33
00:01:38.561 --> 00:01:41.440
and apply F transpose instead.

34
00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:44.784
This time the arrows land on three

35
00:01:44.784 --> 00:01:46.455
one and two, one.

36
00:01:46.455 --> 00:01:48.777
Those are the rows of F

37
00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:51.564
the rows of F are the columns

38
00:01:51.657 --> 00:01:53.050
of F transpose.

39
00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:55.218
To undo F transpose

40
00:01:55.218 --> 00:01:58.126
use the transpose of F inverse.

41
00:01:58.126 --> 00:02:02.034
The plane slides back onto its faint copy.

42
00:02:02.125 --> 00:02:05.033
So the inverse of a transpose is

43
00:02:05.124 --> 00:02:07.760
the transpose of the inverse.

44
00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:11.271
One more use of the transpose.

45
00:02:11.271 --> 00:02:13.421
A vector is a column

46
00:02:13.421 --> 00:02:16.059
so its transpose is a row

47
00:02:16.059 --> 00:02:20.260
and a row times a column is a dot product.

48
00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:23.900
With prices of two, three and four euros

49
00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:27.029
Monday's takings are twelve times two

50
00:02:27.029 --> 00:02:29.035
plus thirty times three

51
00:02:29.035 --> 00:02:30.801
plus five times four.

52
00:02:30.801 --> 00:02:33.930
That is one hundred thirty four euros.

53
00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:38.801
The café also records what each drink costs.

54
00:02:38.801 --> 00:02:42.506
Put price and cost side by side in B

55
00:02:42.506 --> 00:02:43.872
three by two.

56
00:02:43.872 --> 00:02:46.310
Then X B is four by two

57
00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:49.430
takings and costs for each day.

58
00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:52.148
Its transpose is two by four.

59
00:02:52.148 --> 00:02:54.598
X transpose is three by four

60
00:02:54.598 --> 00:02:57.292
and B transpose is two by three.

61
00:02:57.292 --> 00:03:00.150
Which order gives the right shape?

62
00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:01.620
Pause and decide.

63
00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:08.322
The tempting answer keeps the order

64
00:03:08.322 --> 00:03:11.010
X transpose times B transpose.

65
00:03:11.096 --> 00:03:14.131
But the four columns of X transpose

66
00:03:14.218 --> 00:03:17.166
meet only two rows of B transpose.

67
00:03:17.166 --> 00:03:18.640
It does not fit.

68
00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:20.793
The order reverses.

69
00:03:20.888 --> 00:03:23.639
B transpose times X transpose

70
00:03:23.734 --> 00:03:27.245
is two by three, times three by four

71
00:03:27.245 --> 00:03:31.230
which gives two by four, the right shape.

72
00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:34.880
A matrix that equals its own transpose

73
00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:36.576
is called symmetric

74
00:03:36.576 --> 00:03:40.130
it is its own mirror image in the diagonal.

75
00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:42.575
The most important source

76
00:03:42.659 --> 00:03:44.586
is R transpose times R.

77
00:03:44.586 --> 00:03:47.519
Reverse the order to transpose it

78
00:03:47.519 --> 00:03:50.200
and you get R transpose R back.

79
00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:51.553
For the café

80
00:03:51.553 --> 00:03:54.100
X transpose X is three by three

81
00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:56.724
one row and one column per drink.

82
00:03:56.801 --> 00:03:58.961
Its entries are dot products

83
00:03:59.039 --> 00:04:00.659
of the drink columns.

84
00:04:00.736 --> 00:04:03.360
Tea with coffee gives one thousand

85
00:04:03.437 --> 00:04:05.058
three hundred thirty

86
00:04:05.058 --> 00:04:07.990
and it appears in both mirror places.

87
00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:12.749
Careful: not every product is symmetric.

88
00:04:12.749 --> 00:04:17.032
Take A with rows one, two and zero, one.

89
00:04:17.137 --> 00:04:19.540
A transpose A has a two

90
00:04:19.644 --> 00:04:21.943
in both mirror places.

91
00:04:21.943 --> 00:04:24.450
Symmetric, as promised.

92
00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:28.241
But A times A has a four above the

93
00:04:28.342 --> 00:04:31.276
diagonal and a zero below it.

94
00:04:31.276 --> 00:04:32.794
Not symmetric.

95
00:04:32.794 --> 00:04:36.134
The rule is about R transpose R

96
00:04:36.134 --> 00:04:38.360
not about any square.

97
00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:40.898
Before training a model

98
00:04:40.898 --> 00:04:42.857
we shuffle the samples

99
00:04:42.857 --> 00:04:46.447
so that their order carries no information.

100
00:04:46.447 --> 00:04:50.119
Here are the coffee sales as arrows: thirty

101
00:04:50.119 --> 00:04:53.628
twenty five, twenty eight and thirty five

102
00:04:53.628 --> 00:04:55.260
Monday to Thursday.

103
00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:59.746
A random shuffle gives the order three, two

104
00:04:59.746 --> 00:05:00.780
zero, one.

105
00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:05.010
Thursday moves to the front, then Wednesday

106
00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:06.890
Monday and Tuesday.

107
00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:09.787
Shuffling rows is multiplying

108
00:05:09.874 --> 00:05:12.158
by a permutation matrix P

109
00:05:12.158 --> 00:05:16.109
the identity with its rows in the new order.

110
00:05:16.109 --> 00:05:18.480
P X is the shuffled table.

111
00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:21.452
How do we undo the shuffle?

112
00:05:21.452 --> 00:05:24.552
Each column of P has a single one

113
00:05:24.552 --> 00:05:26.501
in a different place.

114
00:05:26.501 --> 00:05:30.045
So P transpose times P is the identity

115
00:05:30.045 --> 00:05:33.943
and the inverse of P is simply P transpose.

116
00:05:33.943 --> 00:05:35.980
Every day slides back.

117
00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:38.635
One warning from practice.

118
00:05:38.635 --> 00:05:42.180
Each day also has a label, the weather

119
00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:44.662
sunny, rainy, sunny, sunny.

120
00:05:44.662 --> 00:05:47.854
Shuffle the labels with the same P

121
00:05:47.854 --> 00:05:50.690
and each day keeps its weather.

122
00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:54.263
Use a different shuffle for the labels

123
00:05:54.263 --> 00:05:55.255
and Monday

124
00:05:55.255 --> 00:05:58.231
with thirty coffees on a sunny day

125
00:05:58.231 --> 00:06:00.131
is now labelled rainy.

126
00:06:00.131 --> 00:06:03.520
The data and the labels no longer match.

127
00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:06.902
So, the transpose is a mirror

128
00:06:06.999 --> 00:06:09.125
image in the diagonal

129
00:06:09.222 --> 00:06:11.541
the rows of A become the

130
00:06:11.638 --> 00:06:13.860
columns of A transpose.

131
00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:17.521
For a product, the order reverses

132
00:06:17.521 --> 00:06:21.320
and R transpose R is always symmetric.

133
00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:25.410
A permutation is undone by its transpose.

134
00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:29.956
Now you try: what is the transpose of A B C?

135
00:06:29.956 --> 00:06:33.771
In part two, each day becomes a vector

136
00:06:33.866 --> 00:06:36.917
and we ask which sets of vectors

137
00:06:37.013 --> 00:06:38.920
you can never leave.
